```

Analysis & Next Steps

Given the clinic.js report and the ResponseAborted errors, the event loop is clearly being blocked under load. The complexity of the middleware seems like the most probable cause.

Do you have any suggestions for how to best debug or profile the middleware itself to identify the exact bottleneck? For instance, is there a way to add more granular logging or performance marks to see which part (authentication, i18n, header application) is consuming the most time?

I am ready to run any further diagnostics or provide additional information that could help us resolve this. Thank you again for your assistance.

Best regards,
Thanks to everyone for responding and taking the time to help me solve the issue.

I made some code adjustments that improved things a bit, but after extensive research, it turned out that the main cause of the high load was bots performing a full site scan at high speed, especially the “Claude AI” bot.

The problem was solved by adding protection measures such as Cloudflare, which reduced the load by more than 90%.

I’m sharing this here in case it helps someone else.

what is the value for webpack.cache.maxAge?
### Summary

I see [here](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/931868b86c4079300d059dab4301e8dab8924249/packages/next/src/build/webpack-config.ts#L2382-L2397) how webpack.cache is created.
I don't see any value for the [maxAge](https://webpack.js.org/configuration/cache/#cachemaxage) parameter.
Can someone confirm that/if in this way the default of 1 month is used?


### Additional information

_No response_

### Example

_No response_
I don't think Next.js sets it:

```ts
import type { NextConfig } from "next";

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  webpack(config) {
    console.log(config.cache);
    return config;
  },
};

export default nextConfig;
```

It doesn't print for any environment here. You could actually set it there if you want, by mutating config.cache.
Next.js itself does not explicitly set a value for `webpack.cache.maxAge`.

By default, webpack uses its own caching behavior when `cache: { type: 'filesystem' }` is enabled. The `maxAge` option is not commonly configured in Next.js setups, so unless you manually override the webpack config, it will rely on webpack’s internal defaults.

If you want to control it, you can extend your Next.js config like this:

module.exports = {
  webpack: (config) => {
    if (config.cache && config.cache.type === 'filesystem') {
      config.cache.maxAge = 1000 * 60 * 60 * 24 * 30; // 30 days
    }
    return config;
  },
};

So in short:
- Next.js does not set `maxAge`
- Webpack default behavior is used
- You can override it manually if needed

useRouter not working in app router component
### Summary

Hi, I need help with navigation in Next.js.

I am using the App Router and trying to navigate to another page after clicking a button, but `useRouter` is not working as I expected.

### Additional information

```js
Here is my code:

import { useRouter } from "next/router";

export default function Page() {
  const router = useRouter();

  return (
    <button onClick={() => router.push("/dashboard")}>
      Go to dashboard
    </button>
  );
}

What I expected:
- when I click the button, it should navigate to /dashboard

What happened instead:
- I get an error or the navigation does not work

Environment:
- Next.js: latest
- Router: App Router
- Node.js: 18+
```


### Example

_No response_
In the App Router, `useRouter` should be imported from `next/navigation`, not `next/router`.

Try this:

```jsx
"use client";

