Hi @youngdriversacademy 

This issue occurs because `useActionState` updates the state **after form submission**, but your `useState` hook doesn’t sync correctly with it by default — causing the controlled `<select>` to reset or flicker.

---

### Problem Summary

- You’re using `useState(group)` to control the select input.
- After form submission, `useActionState` returns the new state from the server (`state.group`), but your local state (`group`) doesn’t update correctly.
- This creates a mismatch between the controlled input and server-updated values.

---

### ✅ Fix 1: Sync State with `useEffect` Safely

Update your `useEffect` to sync `group` only when it's different:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (state.group !== group) {
    setGroup(state.group);
  }
}, [state.group]);
```

This avoids infinite loops and ensures your select value reflects the new server state after submission.

---

### ✅ Fix 2: Controlled-on-Client, Initialized-from-Server

If you want full control on the client, but initialize from server state only once:

```tsx
const [group, setGroup] = useState<string>("");

useEffect(() => {
  if (group === "" && state.group) {
    setGroup(state.group);
  }
}, [state.group]);
```

This treats the input as client-controlled but uses `state.group` only as the initial value after server updates.

---

###  Why This Happens

- `useActionState` returns a new object each time you submit the form.
- Your component re-renders with the new state, but `useState` does **not reinitialize** unless told to.
- Since you're using a controlled `<select>`, its `value` must be kept in sync manually with new props/state.

---

### ✅ Final Working Example

```tsx
"use client";

import { useActionState, useEffect, useState } from "react";
import { saveFormData } from "./actions";

type FormState = {
  group: string;
};

const defaultState: FormState = {
  group: "Junior",
};

export default function FormExample() {
  const [state, formAction] = useActionState(saveFormData, defaultState);
  const [group, setGroup] = useState(state.group);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (state.group !== group) {
      setGroup(state.group);
    }
  }, [state.group]);

  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <label>
        Group:
        <select
          name="group"
          value={group}
          onChange={(e) => setGroup(e.target.value)}
        >
          <option value="Mini">Mini (11–12)</option>
          <option value="Junior">Junior (12–14)</option>
          <option value="Senior">Senior (15+)</option>
        </select>
      </label>

      <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

Hi,

There's a bug with the select input not opting out from form reset behavior.

- https://github.com/facebook/react/issues/30580
- https://github.com/facebook/react/issues/29034

I found a hack on that thread though:

```tsx
        <select
          key={state.group}
          name="group"
          defaultValue={state.group}
          onChange={(e) => setGroup(e.target.value)}
        >
```

Using `key` and `defaultValue` works because, the key change destroys the component identity - https://react.dev/learn/preserving-and-resetting-state#option-2-resetting-state-with-a-key

