Support custom `HTTP Status Code` for Server Components
<details><summary>Previous Proposal</summary>
<p>

### Goals

Manage custom `statusCode` with Server Components in App Router which was an available option in Page Router.
I'm assuming that the most use cases will be error handling.

### Handling `statusCode` in Page Router Server Components

```ts
export async function getServerSideProps() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('https://...');
    const data = await response.json();

    // Customize the response status based on conditions
    const statusCode = response.status !== 200 ? response.status : 404;

    return {
      props: {
        data,
        statusCode,
      },
    };
  } catch (error) {
    return {
      props: {
        data: null,
        statusCode: 500,
      },
    };
  }
}
```

### Suggestion - Pass customized `status`

```ts
import { notFound, setStatus } from 'next/navigation'
// other imports...

async function fetchUser(id) {
  const res = await fetch(`https://...`)
  if (!res.ok) return { status: res.status } // or customized status
  const data = res.json()
  return { status: 200, ...data }
}

export default async function Profile({ params }) {
  const user = await fetchUser(params.id)
  const { status } = user
 
  if (status === 404) notFound()

  // the status code will be most likely 400~500 but should not trigger the Error Boundary
  // and able to return custom components
  if (status === 401) {
    setStatus(status) // or add fallback option?
    return (<NotAuthorized />)
  }
  // ...
}
```

### Reference

- [Page Router - Response Helpers](https://nextjs.org/docs/pages/building-your-application/routing/api-routes#response-helpers)

### Background

Duplicate requests served on the Discussions community.

#50149  #50383 #50906 #52378 #52796

</p>
</details> 

# UPDATE - 21SEP2023

Due to the concept of Streaming, the initial proposal cannot be resolved as @timneutkens has mentioned in https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/53225#discussioncomment-6562537.

Therefore as @icyJoseph has suggested in https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/53225#discussioncomment-6574006, prefetching & setting the status before streaming seems to be a better approach.

## New Proposal - `prefetchStatus`

### Basic Usage

```ts
type Props = {
  params: { id: string }
  searchParams: { [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }
}

export async function prefetchStatus({
  params,
  searchParams,
}: Props): Promise<{ status: number }> {
  // fetch to get status
  const response = await fetch(`https://.../${params.id}`)

  // return status
  return { status: response.status }
}
```

### Custom Status Code

```ts
export async function prefetchStatus() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://...`)
    const data = await response.json()

    if (!data.isAdmin) return { status: 401 }
    if (data.isGone) return { status: 410 }
    // ...

    return { status: response.status }
  } catch (error) {
    // ...
    return { status: 500 }
  }
}
```

### Caution ⚠️

Requires a custom fallback in order to not be caught on the Error/NotFound Boundary.

```tsx
import NotAuthorized from '@components/ui'

export async function prefetchStatus() {
// ...
}

export default function Home() {
  const response = await fetch(`https://...`)
  const data = await response.json()

  if (response.status === 401) return <NotAuthorized />

  return // ...
}
```

### Suggestion - Use with Route Handlers

```ts
// app/api/.../route.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'

export async function GET() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://...`)
    const data = await response.json()

    if (!data.isAdmin) return NextResponse.json({ data }, { status: 401 })
    // ...

    return NextResponse.json({ data })
  } catch (error) {
    // ...
    return NextResponse.json({ error }, { status: 503 })
  }
}
```

```tsx
// app/page.tsx
import { NotAuthorized, ServiceUnavailable } from '@components/ui'

export async function prefetchStatus() {
  const response = await fetch(`https://...`)
  return { status: response.status }
}

export default function Home() {
  const response = await fetch(`https://...`)
  const data = await response.json()

  if (response.status === 401) return <NotAuthorized />
  if (response.status === 503) return <ServiceUnavailable />
  return // ...
}
```

### Background

Although [Status Codes](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/routing/loading-ui-and-streaming#status-codes) have stated that the status codes don't affect SEO;

> This does not affect SEO.

feels like an irony to me that [Learn Next.js](https://nextjs.org/learn/seo/crawling-and-indexing/status-codes) do suggests some status codes that are meaningful in SEO context.

> There are many status codes, but only a handful are meaningful in an SEO context.

### Reference

- [Loading UI and Streaming](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/building-your-application/routing/loading-ui-and-streaming)
- [Learn Next.js - What are HTTP Status Codes?](https://nextjs.org/learn/seo/crawling-and-indexing/status-codes)
- [generateMetadata function](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/generate-metadata#generatemetadata-function)

### Background

Duplicate requests served on the Discussions community.

#50149  #50383 #50906 #52378 #52796

@devjiwonchoi `setStatus` would be great!
While I understand the request. It seems there is a misunderstanding about when streaming starts and how that relates to the status code and your code. This proposal can't work because of limitations with streaming, specifically you can't set the status code after the headers have been sent and the stream has started.

At the point where the `Profile` component in the example runs the headers have already been sent and the stream has been started. This means that you can't change the status code / headers at that point. What we do for `notFound()` / `redirect()` when they are called when the stream has started is adding meta tags recommended by Google for these cases. 

TLDR: because of streaming rendering and the preinitialization (preamble part of the request) in React you can't modify the status / headers, that's also why you can't set cookies / set headers during server components rendering.
