In Next.js App Router, the recommended approach for cache invalidation across multiple instances depends on your setup:

Option 1 — revalidateTag() or revalidatePath() in a Route Handler (recommended):
Create an API endpoint that triggers revalidation:

ts
// app/api/revalidate/route.ts
import { revalidateTag } from 'next/cache';
import { NextRequest } from 'next/server';
export async function POST(request: NextRequest) {
  const tag = request.nextUrl.searchParams.get('tag');
  if (tag) revalidateTag(tag);
  return Response.json({ revalidated: true });
}
Then call this endpoint from your CMS, webhook, or backend whenever data changes. All instances behind your load balancer will revalidate.

Option 2 — On-demand ISR via webhook:
If you're on Vercel, their infrastructure handles propagating revalidation signals across all instances automatically when you call revalidateTag() or revalidatePath().

Option 3 — Self-hosted with Redis:
For self-hosted multi-instance setups, use a custom cache handler with Redis as a shared cache store. Set it in next.config.js:

js
module.exports = {
  cacheHandler: require.resolve('./cache-handler.js'),
}
This ensures all instances share the same cache state.

