Hey @corysimmons — yeah, this is a known pain point. You've already found the right workaround, but let me add some context on *why* this happens and a couple of alternatives.

## Why the manifest ends up in `<body>`

When you use `manifest.ts` (or the `manifest` field in `metadata`), Next.js generates the `<link rel="manifest">` tag as part of the **metadata resolution** process. With RSC and streaming, metadata is resolved asynchronously and injected via the RSC payload — which means it gets streamed into the document *after* the initial HTML shell.

The initial shell contains the `<head>` with whatever static content is there, and then the body starts streaming. By the time the metadata resolves and the `<link rel="manifest">` is injected, the browser's parser has already moved past `<head>` into `<body>`. The browser doesn't move it back up — it just stays wherever it lands in the DOM.

Chrome (and other browsers) specifically look for `<link rel="manifest">` inside `<head>` per the [Web App Manifest spec](https://www.w3.org/TR/appmanifest/#obtaining). If it's in `<body>`, it's technically non-conformant and some browsers just ignore it.

## Your workaround is correct

What you're doing is the right fix:

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export default function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <head>
        <link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.json" />
      </head>
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

By placing the `<link>` directly in the `<head>` of your root layout, it's part of the **static shell** that gets sent immediately — before any streaming happens. Chrome will always see it.

## Alternative: use `manifest.webmanifest` in `/public`

If you want to keep things simple and avoid `manifest.ts` altogether:

1. Put your `manifest.webmanifest` (or `manifest.json`) in the `/public` directory
2. Reference it directly in your root layout's `<head>` as shown above

This way you don't rely on Next.js metadata resolution at all for the manifest.

## If you still want dynamic manifests

If your manifest needs to be dynamic (e.g., different `start_url` per locale), you can create a Route Handler:

```ts
// app/manifest.json/route.ts
export async function GET() {
  const manifest = {
    name: 'My App',
    short_name: 'App',
    start_url: '/',
    display: 'standalone',
    // ... other fields
  };

  return new Response(JSON.stringify(manifest), {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/manifest+json' },
  });
}
```

And still reference it with a static `<link>` in the root layout `<head>`.

## Related issues

This is closely related to the general problem of streamed metadata not being in `<head>` for initial HTML parse. There have been discussions about it:
- [#57087](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/57087) — metadata not in initial HTML
- [#64961](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/pull/64961) — improvements to metadata streaming

Hopefully the Next.js team will address this more holistically, but for now the direct `<link>` in `<head>` is the reliable solution.

Hope this clears things up!

