Caching images from CDN with signed urls
## Context

Trying to fetch images from CDN (Azure Front Door + Azure Blob Storage) using signed urls.
So, the url looks something like this:
```
https://cdn.company.com/path/to/file?p1=val1&p2=val2&etc=more
```

So there is path to file + the query params that contain necessary keys to access the file.

In order to speed up UX, I want these images cached by nextjs.

## Problem

The problem is that the images always miss the cache. I assume it's because the query params (the keys) are always unique. I need to ignore the access params in caching strategy, so it only caches by file path.

## Research

I found this discussion: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/31601

The OP's question is exactly what I mean. The accepted answer by leerob, however, is a bit confusing. The remote patterns are in order for the app to block/allow assets from remote sources. It has nothing to do with caching, right?

## Additional information

The images are returned with this header:
```
Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate
x-nextjs-cache: MISS
```

When I reuse same signed url to cdn, then cache hits.
```
x-nextjs-cache: HIT
```

So I guess it confirms all my suspicions. The cache works, it's just the cache key is always unique, forcing cache miss?

## Potential Fix

Of course, I imagine proxying image via api route would fix it. So I pass in just file path and generate signed url inside handler, then redirect to cdn. This gives nextjs asset url with only file path, without params. But it is suboptimal solution. For example, it makes it much harder to handle authz. Different assets require different permissions in different contexts. So I loose this control.
Yeah this is because the image optimizer's cache key includes the full URL - query params and all. I had a look at the [source](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/packages/next/src/server/image-optimizer.ts) and it hashes everything:

```typescript
static getCacheKey({ href, width, quality, mimeType }) {
    return getHash([CACHE_VERSION, href, width, quality, mimeType])
}
```

So every unique signed URL produces a different cache key even for the same underlying image. There's no config to change this - `remotePatterns` only controls which URLs are allowed, not how the cache key is built.

The most reliable workaround I've seen is proxying through an API route - fetch the image server-side through something like `/api/image?path=container/blob.jpg` that strips the auth params before the optimizer sees it. You'd move the URL signing into the route handler so it happens server-side. That way the optimizer always sees the same URL for the same blob.

Alternatively, if Azure Front Door's signing supports it, you could generate signatures with a fixed time window (e.g. round the expiry to the nearest hour). Same window = same query params = same cache key. Simpler but you get cache churn at window boundaries.
Adding another approach that might work better for your use case, since the API route proxy loses your authz flexibility and the fixed time window has cache churn:
Custom loader with path-only caching
Instead of proxying the full image through an API route, you can use a custom loader that normalizes the URL before it reaches the optimizer. The idea is to split the concerns: let Next.js cache by path only, and resolve the signed URL at request time.

```
// next.config.js
module.exports = {
  images: {
    loader: "custom",
    loaderFile: "./lib/image-loader.ts",
  },
};
```

```
// lib/image-loader.ts
export default function cdnLoader({
  src,
  width,
  quality,
}: {
  src: string;
  width: number;
  quality?: number;
}) {
  // src = just the path, e.g. "/container/blob.jpg"
  // Route through your API that signs on the fly
  return `/api/cdn-image?path=${encodeURIComponent(src)}&w=${width}&q=${quality || 75}`;
}
```
```
// app/api/cdn-image/route.ts
import { NextRequest } from "next/server";

export async function GET(req: NextRequest) {
  const path = req.nextUrl.searchParams.get("path");
  // Apply your authz logic here — check session, permissions, context, etc.
  const signedUrl = generateSignedUrl(path, req);
  
  // Fetch and stream the image (so the optimizer caches by your clean URL)
  const response = await fetch(signedUrl);
  return new Response(response.body, {
    headers: {
      "Content-Type": response.headers.get("Content-Type") || "image/jpeg",
      "Cache-Control": "public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate",
    },
  });
}
```

Then in your components you just pass the clean path:
```
<Image src="/container/blob.jpg" width={800} height={600} alt="..." />
```
The key difference from a naive proxy is that your authz logic stays in the API route — you have full access to the request context (cookies, headers, session) to decide permissions per asset, per user, per context. You're not losing any control; you're just moving the signing to where it belongs (server-side) while giving the optimizer a stable cache key.
Why this is better than the fixed time window approach: no cache churn at window boundaries, no wasted cache entries for the same image, and you don't need Azure Front Door to support time-rounded signatures.
One more thing: if you want to avoid the streaming overhead entirely, there's an open feature request to allow customizing the cache key function in the image optimizer. Might be worth upvoting or opening one if it doesn't exist — the fix in source would be trivial (allow a cacheKeyFn in images config that strips query params), but as nathanschram pointed out, there's no config for it today.

The API route proxy approach would work for cache stability, but it means your Next.js server becomes a trusted cache for these images. The optimizer cache doesn't know about per-user permissions, once an image is cached, any subsequent request for that path gets served without hitting the CDN's auth layer. 

You mention "different assets require different permissions in different contexts," so is it acceptable that, after the first authorized fetch, the image is effectively semi-public on your server? Right now the high cardinality of signatures accidentally prevents this, since every request is a cache miss. If your paths are GUIDs/UUIDs that adds obscurity, but that's not the same as access control.

Also worth noting that the optimizer fetching from your own API route is a self-fetch (loopback), which can be problematic, add latency, etc... like you'd need a an absolute URL to call back into.

The custom loader approach which bypasses the built-in optimizer entirely, which means your API route is now responsible for serving image bytes. You keep auth control (you can check session/permissions in the route), but you lose Next.js's automatic format conversion and resizing, unless you somehow reimplement that.

Fixed time-window signatures, could work but it is that question again, is it ok to weaken the signing to make URLs cacheable. _**Perhaps I am misreading something here altogether.**_

Does Azure Front Door (or your CDN) provide image resizing/format conversion? If so, you could use a [custom loader](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/components/image#loaderfile) that points directly at the CDN with signed URLs, which skips the Next.js optimizer entirely. The client fetches from the CDN with a fresh signature each time, which keeps the auth model intact. You lose Next.js' image server-side caching, in hopes that the CDN serves fast anyway, but if the intent behind signing is to keep images ephemeral/protected, that might be the right trade-off.

This feels less like a Next.js caching problem and more like an architecture decision. Yes Next.js could provide a way to drop certain query params and such, or maybe start using a Vary header-like thing to cache, but, are these images meant to be private (per-user, per-context auth) or semi-public (any authenticated user can see any image)?

Allow blocking route for proper HTTP redirects when cacheComponents is enabled
Problem:
Currently, in Next.js App Router, enabling `cacheComponents: true` forces runtime data access to happen inside `<Suspense>`. This prevents proper HTTP redirects from external backends when the redirect depends on runtime searchParams. Instead, the page initially returns HTTP 200, and the redirect occurs as a streamed/meta redirect, which is not a true HTTP 30x response.

To Reproduce:

1. Enable `cacheComponents: true` in your Next.js App Router configuration.
2. Create a page that wraps a runtime component in <Suspense>.
3. The runtime component reads `searchParams` and calls a backend endpoint that may return a 30x redirect.
4. Call `redirect()` or `permanentRedirect()` inside the Suspense-wrapped component.
5. Start the app (next dev) and request the page with searchParams that trigger the backend redirect.

Observed behavior:
The page returns HTTP 200 first.
Then a streamed/meta redirect occurs instead of a proper HTTP 30x.

Example reproduction:
```tsx
// app/jobs/page.tsx
import { Suspense } from "react";
import { JobsRuntime } from "./jobs-runtime";

export default function Page({ searchParams }: { searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string }> }) {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <JobsRuntime searchParams={searchParams} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```
```tsx
// app/jobs/jobs-runtime.tsx
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";

export async function JobsRuntime({ searchParams }: { searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string }> }) {
  const params = await searchParams;

  // Backend returns 30x depending on searchParams
  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/jobs?filter=${params.filter}`);

  if (res.status === 301) {
    permanentRedirect(res.headers.get("Location")!); // redirect inside Suspense
  }

  return <div>Jobs content</div>;
}
```

Current Behavior:
With `cacheComponents: true`, Next.js forces Suspense for runtime access.
The page returns HTTP 200 first.
Redirect occurs as a streamed/meta redirect.

Expected Behavior:
Next.js should allow blocking route (i.e., await outside of `<Suspense>`) when a redirect depends on runtime data.
`redirect()` / `permanentRedirect()` should result in a proper HTTP 30x response.
This is a fundamental constraint of how streaming works with `redirect()`. Once a Suspense boundary starts streaming, the HTTP headers (including the status code) are already committed as 200 - so `redirect()` can't go back and change them to a 30x. Instead it falls back to inserting a `<meta http-equiv="refresh">` tag into the stream.

Looking at the Next.js source, `redirect()` throws a `RedirectError`, and when that error is caught during streaming, [`make-get-server-inserted-html.tsx`](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/main/packages/next/src/server/app-render/make-get-server-inserted-html.tsx) handles it by injecting the meta tag. The [redirect() docs](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/redirect) note this too: "When used in a streaming context, this will insert a meta tag to emit the redirect on the client side."

With `cacheComponents: true`, your `searchParams` access forces the component into Suspense, which triggers streaming - so you'll always hit this path.

The workaround that should give you proper HTTP 30x redirects is to move the redirect logic into middleware (`proxy.ts` in Next.js 16):

```typescript
// proxy.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'
import type { NextRequest } from 'next/server'

export async function middleware(request: NextRequest) {
  if (request.nextUrl.pathname === '/jobs') {
    const filter = request.nextUrl.searchParams.get('filter')
    if (filter) {
      const res = await fetch(
        `https://api.example.com/jobs?filter=${filter}`,
        { redirect: 'manual' }
      )
      if (res.status === 301) {
        return NextResponse.redirect(
          res.headers.get('Location')!,
          { status: 301 }
        )
      }
    }
  }
  return NextResponse.next()
}

export const config = {
  matcher: '/jobs',
}
```

Middleware runs before rendering starts, so it can set HTTP headers properly. The trade-off is that you're moving backend logic out of the page component, but for redirect-only checks it's a clean fit.

Worth noting this is a known gap the team is tracking - Dan Abramov opened [#86739](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/86739) about the same pattern ("how to intentionally block in Cache Components"), and [#84196](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/issues/84196) covers the `loading.tsx` variant of the same issue.

Let me know if the middleware approach works for your case.

