Cache components enable
### Summary

I enabled cache components in my nextjs 16 app and in my root layout I use the shadcn sidebar with the sidebar open value stored in cookies so in the layout I use `<ProviderWrapper>` where I get the cookie and pass it to sidebarProvider but I get this error that I should wrap the app in a `<Suspense>` but then I have to pass a fallback and me I just want to show the sidebar normally. I really don't know what to do, is someone have a solution?

### Additional information

_No response_

### Example

_No response_
This is expected behavior when **cache components are enabled**.

With cache components on, the **root layout is static by default**. Reading cookies inside the layout makes it **dynamic/async**, so React requires a **Suspense boundary**. This isn’t about showing a loader — it’s just marking an async boundary.

You have two valid options:

### **Recommended: move cookie-based UI state to a Client Component**

Sidebar open/close is a UI preference and doesn’t need to live in a cached layout. Handling it on the client avoids Suspense entirely and works cleanly with shadcn/ui.

### **Alternative: keep cookies in the layout and add Suspense**

If you must read cookies in the layout, wrap the provider:

```tsx
<Suspense fallback={null}>
  <ProviderWrapper>{children}</ProviderWrapper>
</Suspense>
```

`fallback={null}` is perfectly fine here.

You can’t read cookies in a cached layout without either **Suspense** or **client-side handling** — this is by design with React 19 + cache components.


There's a way. Though I don't like it that much, I'd argue that giving up on prerendering the sidebar, because you store whether or not the sidebar is initially opened or closed, is not necessarily a good idea. I do think one should try to prerender as much as possible. Otherwise, layout shifts, or flashes of unstyled/unwanted UI might happen.

> [!NOTE]
>
> Do not overuse this pattern. This helps because it allows you to prerender one state, immediately serve that, and also patch the UI to reconcile with browser state. It works best when all you need to do is modify the html/body tags for theming.


Say for example you have this function:

```tsx
function hydrateSidebarState(cookieName: string) {
  const cookie = document.cookie
    .split("; ")
    .find((row) => row.startsWith(`${cookieName}=`));
  const isOpen = cookie ? cookie.split("=")[1] === "true" : true;
  const state = isOpen ? "expanded" : "collapsed";
  const el = document.querySelector('[data-slot="sidebar"]:not([data-mobile])');
  if (el) {
    el.setAttribute("data-state", state);
    el.setAttribute(
      "data-collapsible",
      state === "collapsed" ? "offcanvas" : ""
    );
  }
}
```

As long as this function has no closures, we are good. We can get a hold of `useServerInsertedHTML` from `next/navigation`, and...

> One could also just inline the script 

```tsx
 useServerInsertedHTML(() => {
    return (
      <script
        dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{
          __html: `(${function hydrateSidebarState(cookieName: string) {
            const cookie = document.cookie
              .split("; ")
              .find((row) => row.startsWith(`${cookieName}=`));

            const isOpen = cookie ? cookie.split("=")[1] === "true" : true;
            const state = isOpen ? "expanded" : "collapsed";
            const el = document.querySelector(
              '[data-slot="sidebar"]:not([data-mobile])'
            );
            if (el) {
              el.setAttribute("data-state", state);
              el.setAttribute(
                "data-collapsible",
                state === "collapsed" ? "offcanvas" : ""
              );
            }
          }.toString()})("${SIDEBAR_COOKIE_NAME}");`,
        }}
      />
    );
  });
```

There's a bunch of assumptions here, most importantly the cookie value resolution and the querySelector, but an app likely doesn't have many sidebars, hopefully.

Then you need to patch the state initializer:

```tsx
 const [_open, _setOpen] = React.useState(() => {
    const cookieValue = getCookie(SIDEBAR_COOKIE_NAME);
    if (cookieValue == null) return defaultOpen;
    return cookieValue === "true";
  });

 useServerInsertedHTML(() => { 
   /* rest of stuff we just showed */
 });
```

And of course, define a `getCookie` (as you had kind of) at top of the module...

```tsx
function getCookie(name: string) {
  if (typeof window === "undefined") return undefined;
  return document.cookie
    .split("; ")
    .find((row) => row.startsWith(`${name}=`))
    ?.split("=")[1];
}
```

And that'll do it. Largely inspired by: https://ethanniser.dev/blog/a-clock-that-doesnt-snap/


