## Background

### TSX/JSX Files, HTML Render Templates in Our Project

Our project is intended to be an API server **ONLY**. We only intentionally serve ONE HTML page on the frontend: the index page which can be reached directly from `https://our-domain.com` - the HTML on this page only verifies that the server is running: ![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a0258f7a-a95c-45ba-9d81-6688e28e9fd0)

We do have ONE `*.tsx` files that is used to render an HTML page which we feed to Puppeteer in order to generate a download-able PDF file. **That's it.** All other `*.tsx` files are related this functionality and only exist **because I've found that they need to** in order for the project to compile successfully:

1. `src/app/layout.tsx`
2. `src/app/global-error.tsx`
3. `src/pages/_error.tsx`
4. `src/app/api/PATH/TO/PDF/SERVICE/pdfReport.tsx`

### Our Project's HTTP Code Based RESTful API Error Handling

The second part of the cause for my `next build` compile-time woes is related to our API's handling of errors... **Note here:** part of the solution is us continuing to "go against the grain" vis Next.js' **requirement that accessing Dynamic Server data in API Route Handlers _should be outside a `try/catch` block so that compile-time rendering can detect errors_**. I don't fully understand how/why this is the case but this is the second part of what we're "doing wrong" in our project (lesson learned!!!) and the following Stackoverflow Answer provided that missing bit of info that allowed me to fix the compile-time error without having to go into **every single API endpoint Route and move uses of `NextRequest.*`** out of the `try/catch` to the top/root of the API Handler function (screenshot: ![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/660a8cba-cbef-4bc5-a311-8fce086fb299)).

[Dynamic server usage: Page couldn't be rendered statically because it used `nextUrl.searchParams` in Next.js version 14](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78010331/dynamic-server-usage-page-couldnt-be-rendered-statically-because-it-used-next)

From the SO Answer:

> The way Next.js knows when dynamic data is used is by throwing custom errors and catch them to switch the renderer method. Simply, when you use the request URL (e.g. request.nextUrl) Next.js internally will throw DynamicServerError and catch it at a top-level.

