## Solution Part 2: If you use `NextRequest.*` data **INSIDE** Try/Catch blocks, be sure to detect and throw Next.js Errors!

We have an Error/Exception handling wrapper class that we use to generate and return HTTP Responses with appropriate HTTP Error Codes. Because we didn't fully understand how Next.js compiles the app and what the implications are when you are using Next.js purely as a RESTful API server, we've composed **all** our dozens of API Routes to wrap **_everything_** in a handler-function top-level `try{} catch(){}` block with our HTTP Error Response composer being the only code in the `catch(){}` block (as mentioned earlier in this post ([@see: SO Answer here](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/78010331/dynamic-server-usage-page-couldnt-be-rendered-statically-because-it-used-next)).

Here's a sample API Route:

```
export async function GET(request: NextRequest) {
  try {
    const searchParams = request.nextUrl.searchParams;
    /* !!! SNIP !!! ---------------------------------------------------------- */
  } catch (error) {
    return HttpExceptionErrorHandlerHelper.handleError(error);
  }
}
```

In order to not have to move the `const searchParams = request.nextUrl.searchParams;` up and out of the `try/catch` into the top and root of the Handler of all our Routes, what I've done is updated our `HttpExceptionErrorHandlerHelper.handleError()` handler like so:

```
import { HttpResponseCompositionHelper } from '@/lib/helpers/httpResponseComposition.helper';
import { isDynamicServerError } from 'next/dist/client/components/hooks-server-context';
import { isHTTPAccessFallbackError } from 'next/dist/client/components/http-access-fallback/http-access-fallback';
import { isHydrationError, isReactHydrationErrorMessage } from 'next/dist/client/components/is-hydration-error';
import { isNextRouterError } from 'next/dist/client/components/is-next-router-error';
import { isRedirectError } from 'next/dist/client/components/redirect-error';
import { isStaticGenBailoutError } from 'next/dist/client/components/static-generation-bailout';

/**
 * This list was found by conducting a search against exported Error Handler functions in the Next.js client
 * components lib itself using the following command:
 *
 *    ```
 *    grep -irE 'export declare function is\\w+Error' /PATH/TO/PROJECT/NODE_MODULES/next/dist/client/components
 *    ```
 */
export class HttpExceptionErrorHandlerHelper {
  public static handleError(error: any): Response {
    if (
      isDynamicServerError(error) ||
      isHTTPAccessFallbackError(error) ||
      isHydrationError(error) ||
      isNextRouterError(error) ||
      isReactHydrationErrorMessage(error) ||
      isRedirectError(error) ||
      isStaticGenBailoutError(error)
    ) {
      throw error;
    }

    const errorMessage = (error?.statusText)
      ? error.statusText
      : (error?.message)
        ? error.message
        : 'unexpected error.';

    if (error.constructor.name === 'Response') {
      errorMessage.toString().replaceAll(/[\r\n\t]/g, '').replaceAll(/\s+/g, ' ');
      console.log(
        `Error encountered when handling request: ${
          JSON.stringify(error.statusText)
        }`,
      );
      return error;
    }

    console.log(
      `Error encountered when handling request: ${errorMessage}`,
      error,
    );
    return HttpResponseCompositionHelper.internalServerError({
      errors: [{ message: `${errorMessage}` }],
      statusText: errorMessage.toString().replaceAll(/[\r\n\t]/g, '').replaceAll(/\s+/g, ' '),
    });
  }
}
```

Please take note of the code comment above that explains how I located and confirmed all **exported `isXYZError()` functions** that Next.js 15 is exporting. Based upon the SO Answer and for lack of time to delve deep into this matter, I've assumed that Next.js (and its server/compiler) need to be able to catch ALL of these errors so, instead of catching/handling these Errors with HTTP Error Code Responses, I've updated this class to "proxy" on these specific Errors so that Next.js itself can handle them.

This, dear reader, was the final step to getting our app to compile successfully again!

## Conclusion

I truly hope that all this detailed information proves useful to future readers and I entreat the Next.js team to help explain what's going on here with some tutorial and/or documentation when/if possible. Official documentation that can be found when these types of compile-time errors occur would be absolutely invaluable and save days of stress for devs who find themselves in the sinking boat I had found myself in.

